WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:05.480
A lot of EDH discourse exists around the concept&nbsp;
of removal, and specifically around the question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:09.640
of whether people should be running more&nbsp;
of it. In that great battle of ideas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:14.640
I fall firmly in the camp of incessantly telling&nbsp;
people to run more removal. I will happily be that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:20.720
annoying guy. However, I also understand why this&nbsp;
advice might not be enjoyable to receive for a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:25.120
of people. In conventional EDH deckbuilding,&nbsp;
ways to interact with your opponents often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:30.560
ends up in the category of vegetables—it’s a&nbsp;
type of card that’s not flashy or exciting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:36.520
but that you need to include regardless. If you’re&nbsp;
building a cool EDH deck around a particular idea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:41.280
sloughing off deck space to spend on interaction&nbsp;
might feel disappointing—you’re effectively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:44.720
diluting the quality of what’s happening&nbsp;
on your side of the board in order to set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:49.600
back opponents. If this line of thinking sounds&nbsp;
like you, I understand that and I’m not here to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:53.000
perform the role of the chiding parent telling&nbsp;
you that you need to grit your teeth and choke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:57.000
down those vegetables. There certainly&nbsp;
are plenty of reasons to run more ways to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:01.160
interact with opponents—the two big ones being&nbsp;
that it leaves you with a better rounded deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:07.240
and it leads to more dynamic and interesting games&nbsp;
rather than luck-based, one-sided stomps. But no,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:12.440
I don’t want to focus on that, because on some&nbsp;
level you probably already know that. Rather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:17.480
I want to offer an alternative vision to the&nbsp;
vegetables analogy: what if you could have all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:23.160
the benefits of running a lot of removal, without&nbsp;
feeling like it’s gobbling up your deck space?

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:26.960
I’m going to talk about a couple methods for&nbsp;
accomplishing this task, and the first method&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:31.600
is to make the slots you’re already spending&nbsp;
on removal do more for you. When we look at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:36.280
most popular removal spells in the EDH format,&nbsp;
it’s mostly single target removal, typically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:40.760
either low-cost options or all-purpose options&nbsp;
that can hit any target. To be clear, these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:46.680
things both absolutely have their place, but they&nbsp;
are an entire deck slot spent removing one card.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:51.000
What if there are two concerning cards on board?&nbsp;
What if somebody plays another one the turn after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:56.080
you remove something? Running enough single target&nbsp;
removal for all potential targets can really tax&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.080 --> 00:02:01.160
your deck space, and there are two alternatives&nbsp;
that can be comparatively compact—multi-removal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:06.520
and removal engines. Multi-removal is exactly&nbsp;
what it sounds like—it’s a spell that removes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:10.520
multiple potential threats. Board wipes are one&nbsp;
example of this, but there are plenty of other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:14.280
options available. Some of these are pretty&nbsp;
straightforward—Hull Breach can destroy both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:18.480
an artifact and an enchantment, and Soul&nbsp;
Shatter forces each opponent to sacrifice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:23.040
a hopefully-valuable creature or planeswalker.&nbsp;
However, as you get to higher mana costs there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:28.360
are some spicier options too. Windgrace’s Judgment&nbsp;
can destroy each opponent’s most valuable nonland&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:33.280
permanent, and both Decimate and Casualties of&nbsp;
War are capable of blasting cards of several card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:38.160
types all at once. A really cool thing about these&nbsp;
is that you’ll typically destroy one or two things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:43.320
that are currently problems, but also a couple&nbsp;
things that might cause problems down the road.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:48.080
A big mana rock, a utility land, or a low-level&nbsp;
value enchantment won’t be priority targets for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:53.200
removal that can only target one thing, but being&nbsp;
able to clean up multiple of these at the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:58.520
time as a higher-value target can really pay off&nbsp;
in the long term in terms of denied advantage.

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:01.760
Removal engines are another way to make&nbsp;
your interaction suite more compact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:06.560
and these are cards that can destroy things over&nbsp;
time. Aura Shards is one of the most powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:11.400
examples of this, but there are also creatures&nbsp;
like Royal Assassin and Trygon Predator that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:15.800
can remove one permanent per turn. Removal&nbsp;
engines can also involve multiple cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:21.240
and you’ll find this most often in control decks.&nbsp;
In my Sharuum Slide deck, the singular control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:25.160
deck in my current lineup of decks, there&nbsp;
are only two cards in the list that could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:30.200
reasonably be described as board wipes. However,&nbsp;
the deck frequently engages in heavy degrees of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:35.640
board suppression through the usage of removal&nbsp;
engines—Astral Slide can recycle explosive ETBs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:40.120
including that of one of the two board wipes, and&nbsp;
there are also a number of ways to get control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:45.000
pieces in and out of the graveyard. The deck&nbsp;
even has a stifle engine—what kind of goofy-ass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:49.880
deck has a stifle engine in it? Other examples&nbsp;
of this might include a pinger paired with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:53.720
deathtouch enabler, Containment Priest paired&nbsp;
with a flicker engine, and Muldrotha paired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:58.680
with a Fleshbag Marauder effect. In general,&nbsp;
these are great for conserving deck space—in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:03.600
toolbox control deck that has networks of ways to&nbsp;
find different cards, a small handful of removal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:07.960
engines can free up a ton of deck space to go&nbsp;
toward counterspells, tech pieces, draw, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:13.720
other categories that might otherwise have to be&nbsp;
a bit leaner to fit an adequate amount of removal.

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:17.440
Now, I should say—in the case of both&nbsp;
multi-removal and removal engines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:21.800
you’re generally going to pay more mana for the&nbsp;
added benefits, as compared to the single-target&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:26.400
counterparts of these cards. And, for the&nbsp;
leanest, most unapologetically proactive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:31.760
decks out there, that extra cost will likely be&nbsp;
an unacceptable downside. But not all decks fit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:37.360
that description—we’ve seen low cost, all-purpose&nbsp;
removal become the quote-unquote staple choice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:41.880
even though plenty of decks absolutely can afford&nbsp;
to pay a little more mana in order to get more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:46.600
out of their removal. Board wipes are the model of&nbsp;
this that most people are familiar with, and these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:51.800
are nice but not perfect. They often destroy your&nbsp;
stuff as well, and another downside with them goes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:57.160
back to the temporality of removal—if you purely&nbsp;
focus on blasting all the creatures off the board,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:01.520
you’ll be safe the turn the board wipe is cast,&nbsp;
but a lot of decks can bounce back shockingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:06.440
quickly from a board wipe, or even mitigate it&nbsp;
entirely. Your opponents have been through this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:10.640
before—you’re not catching some doe-eyed Timmy&nbsp;
off guard with the first Wrath of God he’s ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:15.400
seen in his life. This isn’t to say you need to&nbsp;
avoid board wipes—they’re a great option for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:19.960
lot of situations—but if you’re considering&nbsp;
your fourth or fifth board wipe for a deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:24.520
maybe consider that there are other options&nbsp;
out there for accomplishing a similar task.

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:29.040
Okay, so that was the method of densifying the&nbsp;
part of your deck that you dedicate to removal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:33.160
The second method is to widen your removal suite,&nbsp;
but to make an effort to pick out removal that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:38.360
blends into the rest of what your deck is doing.&nbsp;
This allows it to feel less space-gobbling even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:42.560
as the number of removal cards in your deck&nbsp;
stays high. One way of doing this is picking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:47.000
out cards that align with your deck’s synergies,&nbsp;
and this is typically easier the more general your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:51.360
deck’s synergies are. One of the most general&nbsp;
options is card type—if you’re running a deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:55.560
based around artifacts or around instants and&nbsp;
sorceries, I can guarantee you that there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:59.640
a ton of options for interaction that will slot&nbsp;
in nicely with the synergy core of your deck.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:04.680
This is similarly doable in decks that focus&nbsp;
around core game mechanics—there are lots of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:09.680
great options for decks based around counters,&nbsp;
tokens, and graveyards. For a few examples of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:13.880
synergistic picks, you could run Drown in&nbsp;
Ichor in a deck with a focus on counters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:17.680
you could run Trygon Predator in a deck&nbsp;
that is looking for evasive attackers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:21.760
and you could run Lethal Scheme in deck that gets&nbsp;
a wide board of creatures and wouldn’t mind a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:27.160
of extra filtering. An extension of this principle&nbsp;
is modal cards—cards like Valorous Stance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.160 --> 00:06:32.720
Silverback Elder, and Gix’s Command are solid for&nbsp;
both removal and non-removal purposes. There are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:36.400
also plenty of cards that are effectively&nbsp;
modal without explicitly invoking the word&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:42.400
“choose”—landcycling cards like Angel of the Ruins&nbsp;
as well as MDFCs come to mind. The important thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:46.920
is that these cards can allow a deck to pack a&nbsp;
lot of removal that can be used when needed, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:52.120
not feel like its deck slots are being actively&nbsp;
gobbled up. This leads to more fluid gameplay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:57.040
since these cards can be lands or draw or ramp&nbsp;
or protection in addition to being removal. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.040 --> 00:07:01.200
is also why counterspells are good if you can&nbsp;
afford to hold up mana without falling behind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:04.920
but this is a video about removal&nbsp;
spells so I’ll leave it at that.

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:09.720
As an example of removal blending in, my friend&nbsp;
Hans has a deck he likes to call Izzet Bizzet—it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:14.400
a Lilah, Undefeated Slickshot deck that runs&nbsp;
as many Prophetic-Bolt-style cards as it can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:19.600
possibly get its hands on—we’re talking any red&nbsp;
blue instant or sorcery that both deals damage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:24.960
and draws cards in some capacity. As a result of&nbsp;
this, even though the deck is packed out the wazoo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:29.680
with creature removal, since almost all of that&nbsp;
removal draws cards and works with the commander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:35.000
as well, it feels perfectly fine. Another example&nbsp;
of this is my Radha deck, and that deck actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:40.160
takes advantage of both methods I’ve mentioned in&nbsp;
this video. For the blending in part, all of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:45.680
removal in this deck is attached to either a big&nbsp;
creature or a cascade spell. This is great because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:50.040
those are things I want anyway—cascade spells&nbsp;
will usually grab me a piece of ramp to pump up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:55.760
my land count, and big creatures are what I use to&nbsp;
pressure life totals and eventually win. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.760 --> 00:08:01.600
compounded by the density of these—a full seven of&nbsp;
these are one-card removal engines, and a single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:07.160
one of these sticking around for multiple turns&nbsp;
can wildly warp the shape of a game. As a result&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.160 --> 00:08:12.200
of these things, the deck is very much so eating&nbsp;
its removal vegetables, but when I play it it just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:17.920
feels like a big dumb cascade ramp deck, even as&nbsp;
it eats opposing permanents at a healthy pace.

00:08:17.920 --> 00:08:22.640
I’ve given a couple of nice options for fitting&nbsp;
more removal into a deck—densifying the existing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:27.720
removal suite through multi-removal or removal&nbsp;
engines can let the same number of cards do more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:31.760
and blending removal into the rest of what&nbsp;
a deck is doing through synergistic or modal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:36.320
options can similarly offer more removal in&nbsp;
practice without it feeling like it’s hogging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:41.680
too much deck space. However putting these&nbsp;
options into practice requires understanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:46.520
what removal is actually doing for your&nbsp;
deck. Even in this, a video about removal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.520 --> 00:08:51.760
I’ve so far largely abstracted away what removal&nbsp;
is actually accomplishing in a game, letting it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:57.720
sit simply assumed that it’s a good thing to have.&nbsp;
So, what is removal doing? For some decks, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.720 --> 00:09:03.320
is a question with a clear, specific answer—in&nbsp;
my Francisco Malcolm deck, a deck that fits the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.320 --> 00:09:08.800
lean-and-unapologetically-proactive descriptor&nbsp;
I mentioned earlier, removal principally exists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:14.280
to kill creatures that threaten my turn 5-6 win&nbsp;
or that might allow opponents to win faster than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.280 --> 00:09:19.200
that. If I’m playing the deck and my opponents&nbsp;
play out some scary engine creatures on turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:24.520
4 to 5, I don’t generally need to remove these&nbsp;
as long as my combo is on track to end the game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.520 --> 00:09:29.520
before those creatures have a chance to cause&nbsp;
problems. For this reason, all of the deck’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:34.600
dedicated removal is 2 mana or less to allow&nbsp;
it to be weaved into a turn comfortably—I run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:39.080
the low-cost staples because I don’t need to&nbsp;
care what’s happening on board two turns from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:44.600
now. Similarly, I run a lot of mana-efficient,&nbsp;
single-target removal in my Meria deck as well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:49.560
because it’s a Voltron deck—player removal is&nbsp;
absolutely on the table, so my removal is there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:55.120
to deal with obstacles to my beatdown rather than&nbsp;
controlling the board. By contrast, I run removal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.120 --> 00:09:59.520
engines in my slower, grindier decks because the&nbsp;
timeframe for winning is a lot less concrete—I’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.200 --> 00:10:04.440
usually have some tools to put pressure on&nbsp;
opponents relatively early, but the plan to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:08.760
win isn’t focused enough to be relied on as&nbsp;
any sort of clock to calibrate the rest of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:14.240
my gameplan against. Thus, in these sorts&nbsp;
of decks, my removal exists to let me stay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:19.560
alive and prevent opponents from winning for some&nbsp;
unknown duration of time, and as a result I need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.560 --> 00:10:24.680
to prepare for a wider range of situations and&nbsp;
run removal that works better in the longer term.

00:10:24.680 --> 00:10:28.400
There will also be the question of instant&nbsp;
speed removal vs sorcery speed removal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.400 --> 00:10:33.120
and this will come to power level above all&nbsp;
else. If you’re playing at higher powers where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:37.520
sudden infinite combo attempts might happen, and&nbsp;
especially if you’re not playing blue to allow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.520 --> 00:10:42.320
for counterspells, instant speed removal will&nbsp;
likely be kind of a must. Likewise, if you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.320 --> 00:10:47.120
holding up removal at instant speed just in case,&nbsp;
you also want that removal to cost as little as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.120 --> 00:10:53.040
possible to avoid wasted mana if the removal&nbsp;
is unneeded. Staples are staples for a reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:57.880
and this is basically the reason. You might even&nbsp;
want to include a handful of these in a slower,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.880 --> 00:11:01.400
mid-power deck just for the sake of&nbsp;
contingencies—they’re good cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:04.800
and if you failed to slow down a player&nbsp;
earlier in their gameplan, having that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:09.680
insurance policy to prevent a blowout can be&nbsp;
important. However, if you’ve already got your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.680 --> 00:11:14.400
first three or four single target staples, maybe&nbsp;
try branching out a bit from there and consider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:23.160
alternatives that work better for your deck in&nbsp;
particular. Your deck will likely thank you.

